Unreleased
---
Feature
- The library opens, with the family's five scales and no control yet. What a
  design language is made of before it draws anything is the vocabulary its
  controls will share, so this is where the corners, the type ramp, the
  elevation levels, the durations and curves, and the semantic colours are
  fixed:

    * the corner scale stops at sixteen and steps by two, because this family
      turns a corner by a few pixels rather than by a quarter of the control;
    * the elevation scale is wide and faint and sits close under the surface,
      which is the proportion that makes a lift read as light rather than as a
      cut-out edge - the opposite trade from a flatter family's tight, dark
      shadow;
    * the curves are symmetric, so a movement leaves and arrives at the same
      rate, which is what travelling across a surface looks like;
    * the semantic colours are the five the system itself answers with, in both
      of the two cuts they come in. They are here rather than on the palette
      because they are not the theme's to choose: a switch is green when it is
      on whatever the theme's accent is, and making that follow the accent
      would be a different design language wearing this one's shapes. Surface,
      text and accent are still read from the palette.

  What this library deliberately does not carry is the other half of the
  likeness: the platform's materials - the blur a sidebar sits on, the way a
  sheet lets the page show through. Those cannot be verified on a machine that
  does not run the platform, so they are not attempted at all, and a shape that
  is close without them is the whole of the goal here rather than a shortfall
  to be filed later.
- `CupertinoSwitch`, a switch drawn as a slim capsule with a white knob in it.
  Qt has a check box and no switch, and the two are not the same control: this
  one says that what it stands for is in force now, where a check box says a
  choice has been made and will be collected later. Two proportions make the
  drawing this family's rather than another's. The track is slim - a bead's
  height taller than the knob, and the knob nearly fills it - so the control
  reads as a line with a bead on it rather than as a filled bar. And the fill
  does not follow the knob: the track is the off grey until the knob has
  crossed the middle and the on green from there, one change rather than a
  blend, which is the half of the likeness a shape can carry without the
  platform's materials. The green is the family's semantic colour and not the
  theme's accent, so a switch stays green under every theme; the off grey is the
  same semantic grey thinned over the page, one alpha that answers both faces.
  The knob is white on both faces, because it reads as the highlight on the
  track rather than as a surface of the page, and the one lift it has is the
  family's raised level. The travel rides on the normal duration, so a theme's
  motion scale reaches it and the reduce-motion switch silences it without the
  control checking either. There is no sentence in the control: in this family
  a switch is the capsule and the words beside it are a label of their own.
- `CupertinoSlider`, a thin rail with a round handle that rides it. Qt's QSlider
  is a style's idea of a slider and every one of them draws a groove the toolkit
  owns; what this one draws is the least a slider can say - a four pixel rail
  with capsule ends, the part the handle has reached in the family's blue and the
  rest in its grey, and no marks under either, because a mark every interval
  would be a second answer to a question the handle has already answered. The
  handle is what is taken hold of, and it says so: seven times the height of the
  rail, lifted off it with the family's raised shadow, and four points wider
  while it is being dragged - the one thing here that moves without a value
  changing. A press anywhere on the rail moves the handle there rather than
  waiting for a drag, since a slider that waited would lie about where the
  pointer is; a press while it is held follows the pointer at once, where a step
  from the keyboard or the wheel travels on the quick duration, because there the
  movement is what tells the user it moved. The keys are the ones every family
  here answers - the arrows, Page Up and Page Down, Home and End - because a
  keyboard is one convention for the whole project rather than one per family.
  The value is also carried to an assistive tool through a value interface, so
  the number can be read back and set back.
- `CupertinoSegmentedControl`, one row of labels with a white pill under the
  picked one. The row is the control: a set of radio buttons would be a stop per
  button and a ring round each of them, where here the whole row is one tab stop,
  the arrow keys walk the pick, and what moves is the pill - the pick is the
  position of a thing rather than the state of one of several. The pill slides on
  the family's normal duration over the lines between the segments, and the line
  it is passing over goes under it for as long as it is there, which is what
  makes the movement read as one thing moving rather than a shape appearing in a
  new cell; that is also why the lines are drawn before the pill instead of being
  skipped for the picked segment. The row is the family's grey thinned over the
  page, the pill is white and raised - the level this family spends on a surface
  that sits on another - and a label that has not been picked is the ink thinned.
  One segment and none at all are states a data change can put it in, and neither
  is a crash: there is simply nothing to move.
- `CupertinoButton`, a word in a capsule at one of the three levels the family
  gives an action. The levels are the whole of what tells one button from
  another - there is no icon, no second line and no arrow: `Filled` is the one
  action on a surface and carries the family's blue at full strength with the
  word reversed out of it, `Tinted` is the ones beside it with the same blue
  thinned into the page and the word in the blue, and `Plain` is an action that
  is only a word. The capsule casts nothing, because a raised thing in this
  family lies on something and a button lies on the page. Pressed, it takes the
  state layer the base draws rather than shrinking: a control that moved under
  the finger would be moving the page. Enter answers on the press and Space on
  the release, the same as the other families here, and what the button says is
  its accessible name for as long as the application has not given it a better
  one.
- CupertinoActivityIndicator, eight spokes fading round a centre - the mark a
  page shows while it waits. There is no track and no arc and no number in it,
  which is what tells it apart from a progress bar: a bar answers "how much
  longer" and this only says "still going", so the spokes are equal, each one
  fades a little more than the one before, and the whole wheel turns. The fade
  is fixed to the spokes rather than to the direction of travel - the wheel
  moves, the light on it does not - which is what the eye reads as a turn. A
  step of the turn is the family's quickest duration and a whole turn is eight
  of them, so the theme's motion scale retimes the wheel and reduce motion
  stops it: the spokes stay drawn, still saying the page is busy, because a
  wait that vanished with the animation would read as a page that had finished.
  Hidden, it stops its timer; shown again, it starts one. The wheel is not
  clickable and is not in the tab order.

Bugfix
- The segmented control's pill is a face standing on the row and not a hole cut
  through it: white on a light page, and on a dark one the family's grey lifted
  off the page. Left white on a dark page the pill was a bright square in the
  middle of the row, and the label - which is the family's light ink there -
  landed white on white, so the pick read as an empty cell. The ink stays the
  palette's, because it is the page's ink; it is the face that has to be a step
  lighter than the row on either page, and white is that step on only one of
  them.
